 Purpose-built biodiversity platforms and aggregators have transformed species-occurrence research, and their photographs increasingly support phenotypic measurements (Laitly et al., 2021; Luong et al., 2023; McKenzie et al., 2026). A complementary opportunity is **iEcology**: extracting ecological information from digital material accumulated online for purposes other than ecological recording (Jarić et al., 2020). Such sources can contain observations that were never submitted as formal species records and can therefore expand the pool of usable ecological imagery.
 
-YAMAP provides one such source for mountain plants. It is primarily a hiking-navigation and activity-diary platform. During a recorded hike, photographs can retain positions along a GPS activity track and can be displayed at those photographed locations when an activity is shared. The unit of organization is therefore not a biodiversity checklist but a mountain activity and route. For a conspicuous flowering plant, this creates a potentially valuable stream of incidental, route-linked photographs across many mountain systems.
+YAMAP provides one such source for mountain plants. It is primarily a hiking-navigation and activity-diary platform. During a recorded hike, photographs can retain positions along a GPS activity track and can be displayed at those photographed locations when an activity is shared. The unit of organization is therefore not a biodiversity checklist but a mountain activity and route. For a conspicuous flowering plant, this creates a potentially valuable stream of incidental, route-linked photographs across many mountain systems. The mountain focus is not only a sampling limitation: for a native wild herb whose geographical variation is concentrated in mountain and semi-natural habitats, it deliberately enriches the part of the landscape most relevant to reconstructing natural trait geography, although it does not guarantee that every photographed plant is wild.
 
-This does not make YAMAP unbiased, nor do we regard it as universally superior to GBIF or iNaturalist. Users choose routes and subjects, trails differ in use, photograph locations can be hidden, GPS is imperfect and conspicuous flowers may be photographed preferentially. Instead, YAMAP changes the observation process: the original user goal is hiking documentation rather than taxon reporting. We therefore combine this source with manual ecological validation. Author review can reduce taxonomic errors, repeated-image problems and mistakes in defining the focal petal region, although it cannot remove route-access or subject-selection bias. The resulting data are best understood as an author-reviewed, GPS-linked hiking-photograph sample.
+The volume of this non-specialist image stream was unexpectedly high. In a matched Supplementary benchmark for Japan in 2023–2025, the author-screened YAMAP retrieval contained 1,964 georeferenced focal-species photo records (1,963 unique image hashes), compared with 516 georeferenced iNaturalist observations with photographs (882 attached photos; 472 Research Grade). The matched GBIF human-observation image set contained 393 records, 389 of which were syndicated from iNaturalist. Thus, even within only three recent years, YAMAP supplied 3.8 times as many georeferenced focal-species records as the matched iNaturalist observation set and more than twice as many study images as all attached iNaturalist photos. These comparisons are taxon- and period-specific rather than platform-wide rankings (Supplementary Information).
+
+This does not make YAMAP unbiased, nor do we regard it as universally superior to GBIF or iNaturalist. Users choose routes and subjects, trails differ in use, photograph locations can be hidden, GPS is imperfect and conspicuous flowers may be photographed preferentially. Instead, YAMAP changes the observation process: users are documenting hikes rather than participating in a focal-species inventory. This may reduce selection conditioned specifically on taxon reporting or research purpose, but it does not remove observer bias in general. We therefore combine the alternative observation process with exhaustive study-specific validation. Author review can reduce taxonomic errors, repeated-image problems and mistakes in defining the focal petal region, although it cannot remove route-access, conspicuousness or subject-selection bias. The resulting data are best understood as an author-reviewed, GPS-linked hiking-photograph sample.
 
